<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jalón</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Jalón is a river of north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, a right-bank tributary of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ebro-river">Ebro</a> that rises in the province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soria">Soria</a> and flows north-east past <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calatayud">Calatayud</a> to join the Ebro near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zaragoza">Zaragoza</a>. Its valley carries the main route between the Ebro basin and the central Meseta, followed by the Roman road and by today's railway and motorway. In antiquity it was the land of Celtiberian peoples such as the Titti, Belli and Lusones.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Jalón, known in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> as the Salo, is one of the principal tributaries of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ebro-river">Ebro</a>. It is 224 kilometres long and drains a basin of 9,338 square kilometres. Its flow at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calatayud">Calatayud</a> averages 20.8 cubic metres per second but is highly irregular because of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> pattern of rainfall. E. Bascuas derives the name from the old European hydronymy and from an old root *sal-, meaning flowing water or stream.</p><p>The river rises in a spring at the foot of the Sierra Ministra, in Esteras de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medinaceli">Medinaceli</a> in the province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soria">Soria</a>, and runs through Arcos de Jalón to Monreal de Ariza in the province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zaragoza">Zaragoza</a>. The Nágima joins at Monreal de Ariza and the Henar at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cetina">Cetina</a>, before the river enters a limestone gorge at Alhama de Aragón. It then crosses Paleozoic country in the Cordillera Ibérica near Bubierca and is joined by the Piedra and the Manubles at Ateca.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>At <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calatayud">Calatayud</a> the valley widens and the Jiloca, Perejiles and Ribota join the river. It then winds through the Sierra de Algairén and the Sierra de la Virgen and, once out of the mountains, enters the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ebro-river">Ebro</a> valley, where the Aranda, Grío and Alpartir join it. It meets the Ebro at Torres de Berrellén, a suburb of the city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zaragoza">Zaragoza</a>. The valley forms the main route of communication between the Castilian Plateau and the Ebro.</p><p>Much of the river's course has been declared a Special Zone for Bird Protection, and its birds include falcons, eagles, vultures, owls, wrens and herons. Poplar, willow and ash grow along it. Carp, trout and eels were economically important in the past and are returning as pollution is combated. In 2007 the Spanish Ministry of the Environment and the governments of Aragon, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a> and León and Castile-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-mancha">La Mancha</a> adopted a river management plan, the Plan Hidrológico del río Jalón.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Jalón (river)” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Unlike many conquerors, the kings let much of the Muslim population stay on the land, and the mudéjares, Muslims living under Christian rule, remained a large part of the rural workforce on the estates of nobles and the military orders, especially in the Ebro and <b>Jalón</b> valleys.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-aragon">Kingdom of Aragon · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The Belli lived in the <b>Jalón</b> and Huerva valleys of modern Zaragoza province from about the 4th century BCE, part of the wider Celtiberian world.</blockquote><a href="/state/belli">Belli</a></li><li><blockquote>The Lusones lived on the high plateau around the sources of the Tagus and the <b>Jalón</b>, between modern Guadalajara and Zaragoza.</blockquote><a href="/state/lusones">Lusones</a></li><li><blockquote>Muhammad received Calatayud, an old town on the <b>Jalón</b> controlling the road from Zaragoza to Toledo.</blockquote><a href="/state/taifa-of-calatayud">Taifa of Calatayud</a></li><li><blockquote>The Titti were one of the Celtiberian peoples of the middle Ebro tributaries, probably around the <b>Jalón</b> valley in modern Zaragoza province.</blockquote><a href="/state/titti">Titti</a></li></ul></section>
